    "markdown": "---\ntitle: Agricultural Census for 1851, Glengarry and Vaudreuil Counties\nauthor: Kenneth Morrison Brown\ndate: August 4, 2026\ndescription: A transcription of the Agricultural Census of Canada of 1851 for two adjacent counties 60–100 km west of Montreal between the St. Lawrence and Ottawa Rivers.  Vaudreuil County in Quebec was settled under seigneurial land tenure with a predominantly French-speaking population and Glengarry County in Ontario was settled under freehold tenure, with a largely Highland Scots-Gaelic-speaking population in 1851.  From the census variables, can we get a coherent picture of the variability of agricultural practices in these two counties?\ncategories:\n  - EDA\n  - data assessment\n  - linear regression\n  - multivariate analysis\n  - logistic regression\n  - clustering\ndata:\n  year: 1852\n  files: GlengarryVaudreuilAgricCensus1851En.csv.gz\n---\n\n## Motivation\n\nIn our time, farms in North America are often large, highly specialized\noperations. But what did farming look like without herbicides, pesticides,\nartificial fertilizers, before mechanization, and before railways were built?\n\nThe Canadian agricultural census of 1851 collected data on the size of holding,\nthe amount of land under cultivation, the amount of land devoted to various\ncrops, as well as production of these crops. Data were also collected on numbers\nof livestock, and on the products of farm enterprise, such as wool and cloth\nproduction, dairy products, and cured beef and pork. These enterprises were\npartly for each family's own needs, but there were also local markets and trade\namongst neighbors. The picture that emerges is one of diversified, and by modern\nstandards, small operations.\n\nIs it possible to find patterns in this diversity and relate these to spatial or\ncultural variables, or to the size and apparent prosperity of the operation? To\nwhat extent were there very wealthy families, or poor families, and can these be\ndetected using the variables measured? The area includes two different types of\nland tenure, and a diversity of cultural traditions. Do these factors have a\ndiscernible effect on farming practices?\n\n## Data\n\nThe Agricultural Censuses of Canada carried out in rural areas were meant to get\nan idea of the state of farming for the census year. To collect the data,\ntrained enumerators traveled through each county to record data about each farm.\nThe 1851 census was meant to cover farming that occurred in that year, but the\nenumeration was actually done in early 1852, so the accuracy of the results does\ndepend on whether farmers kept good records.\n\nEach row in the data represents a household and its land, and variables include\nthe size of holding, both total and under cultivation (at this time, there was\nstill land to be cleared), production of various crops, numbers of livestock and\nproduction from other farm enterprises (e.g., wool, cloth, butter, cheese, maple\nsugar). Not every row represents a farm, as there were villages, as well as\nrural households with livelihoods other than farming.\n\nThis extract covers Glengarry and Vaudreuil Counties, which are 60–100 km west\nof Montreal between the St. Lawrence and Ottawa Rivers. Vaudreuil County in\nQuebec was settled under [seigneurial land\ntenure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seigneurial_system_of_New_France) with a\npredominantly French-speaking population and Glengarry County in Ontario was\nsettled under [freehold tenure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freehold_(law)),\nwith a largely Highland Scots-Gaelic-speaking population in 1851.\n\nThere are 4,632 households in the data. Data collection was by local\nenumerators, and there is evident variability in the way the data were\ncollected, resulting in missing data for some variables.\n\n<h4><a href=\" https://cmustatistics.github.io/data-repository/data/GlengarryVaudreuilAgricCensus1851En.csv.gz \"> GlengarryVaudreuilAgricCensus1851En.csv.gz </a></h4>\n<div d