ipt>\n</div>\n\n### Variable descriptions\n\n| Variable | Description |\n|-----------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| IndexNoA | Observation number. |\n| IndexNoCD | PageNo + LineNo/100. PageNo and LineNo are explained below. Of the form 137.01. This index is useful for locating data in the original manuscripts. |\n| Name | Given name of the proprietor. |\n| Surname | Surname of the proprietor. |\n| NameOrigin | Ethnicity/language assignment primarily based on Surname. E = English, F = French, I = Irish, O = Other, S = Scots. See Supplementary Materials, Part A. |\n| Concession | Concession name or number. See Supplementary Materials, Part B, Concession, Lot, Location, Latitude and Longitude |\n| Lot | Lot number, or number of lots, or fraction of a lot. See Supplementary Materials, Part B. This variable was treated differently depending on the type of land tenure. |\n| Location | Name given to the area on which latitude and longitude were determined. For Glengarry, this is the individual farm, and this is shown as Location = Farm. For Vaudreuil, the location is based on the Concession name. See Supplementary Materials, Part B. |\n| Latitude | Latitude of Location. See Supplementary Materials, Part B. |\n| Longitude | Longitude of Location. See Supplementary Materials, Part B. |\n| LandTotal | Total number of acres of land held by the proprietor. In the curated data all of measures of land area are in acres. For most of Vaudreuil County, the original land data measure was the *arpent.* These have been translated to acre equivalents. This comment applies to all of the land variables. See Supplementary Materials, Part C: Units of Measurement. |\n| LandCultivated | Number of acres of land under cultivation. |\n| LandCrops1851 | Number of acres under crops in 1851. |\n| LandPasture1851 | Number of acres in pasture in 1851. |\n| LandGardenOrchard | Number of acres devoted to gardens or orchards in 1851. |\n| LandWoodWild | Number of acres of land undeveloped, wild or wood. For the six land variables listed, LandTotal = LandCultivated + LandWoodWild, and LandCultivated = LandCrops1851 + LandPasture1851 + LandGardenOrchard |\n| LandWheat | Number of acres planted in wheat. |\n| ProdWheat | Production of wheat in bushels. For most of Vaudreuil County, the original produce measure was the *minot.* These have been translated to bushel equivalents. See Supplementary Materials, Part C: Units of Measurement. This comment applies to all of the production variables where production is by volume. It is understood that all the production measures refer to the year 1851. |\n| LandBarley | Number of acres planted in barley. |\n| ProdBarley | Production of barley in bushels. |\n| LandRye | Number of acres planted in rye. |\n| ProdRye | Production of rye in bushels. |\n| LandPeas | Number of acres planted in peas. |\n| ProdPeas | Production of peas in bushels. |\n| LandOats | Number of acres planted in oats. |\n| ProdOats | Production of oats in bushels. |\n| LandBuckwheat | Number of acres planted in buckwheat. |\n| ProdBuckwheat | Production of buckwheat in bushels. |\n| LandMaize | Number of acres planted in maize. |\n| ProdMaize | Production of maize in bushels. |\n| LandPotatoes | Number of acres planted in potatoes. |\n| ProdPotatoes | Production of potatoes in bushels. |\n| LandTurnip | Number of acres planted in turnips. |\n| ProdTurnip | Production of turnips in bushels. |\n| ProdClover | Production of clover, timothy, and other grass in bushels. |\n| ProdCarrots | Production of carrots in bushels. |\n| ProdMangelWurzel | Production of mangelwurzel in bushels. |\n| ProdBeans | Production of beans in bushels. |\n| ProdHops | Production of hops in pounds. |\n| Foin | Production of hay in \"bundles\" (*bottes*). Bundles were the more common measure in French language areas, and the Census report of 1855 reports quantities in both units using the conversion: one ton = 140 *bottes.* See Supplementary Materials, Part C. |\n| Hay | Production of hay in tons. See the comment under Foin |\n| ProdFlax | Production of flax or hemp in pounds. |\n| Tobacco | Production of tobacco in pounds. |\n| Wool | Production of wool in pounds. |\n| Apples | Production of apples in bushels. The production of apples was not recorded in all Subdistricts. Enumerators were given three \"blank\" columns to record produce specific to their own Subdistrict. For Glengarry and Vaudreuil, recording production of apples was the most common use of the blank columns. |\n| MapleSugar | Production of maple sugar in pounds. |\n| FulledCloth | Production of fulled cloth in yards. |\n| ProdLinen | Production of linen in yards. |\n| Flannel | Production of flannel in yards. |\n| BullsOxenSteers | Number of bulls, oxen or steers. |\n| MilchCows | Number of milk cows. (The English language name of the variable follows the 1851 usage. In French, *vaches laitières*) |\n| Calves | Number of calves or heifers. |\n| Horses | Number of horses of all ages. |\n| Sheep | Number of sheep. |\n| Pigs | Number of pigs. |\n| Butter | Production of butter in pounds. |\n| Cheese | Production of cheese in pounds |\n| Beef | Production of beef, measured in hundredweight (Cwt). See Supplementary Materials, Part C: Units of Measurement for discussion. |\n| Pork | Production of pork, measured in hundredweight (Cwt). See Supplementary Materials, Part C: Units of Measurement for discussion. |\n| ValueProperty | Estimate of property value in pounds sterling made by the enumerators for the SubDistrict of St.-Polycarpe in Vaudreuil. For all of the other SubDistricts, this column is `NA`. The intended use of this column in the Census manuscripts was to record the quantity of dried fish produced; only one household produced dried fish. |\n| County | Name of county. Glengarry, Vaudreuil See Supplementary Materials, Part E |\n| SubDistrict | Name of Subdistrict. See Supplementary Materials Part E. The full names of the Subdistricts are used. |\n| SubDistrictNoA | Within each county, each Subdistrict was given a number by the transcriber. |\n| DistrictNo | Census number for District. Glengarry = 10, Vaudrreuil = 34 |\n| SubDistrictNoB | Census number for SubDistrict as assigned in the original Census. See Supplementary Materials, Part E |\n| PageNo | Page number in the original manuscript census. The page number and line number are useful for checking the transcribed data with the original manuscript. See also IndexNoCD above. |\n| LineNo | Line number on the page in the original manuscript. Each page had 50 lines. |\n| IndexNoB | Index number within a SubDistrict. For example, for Lancaster SubDistrict, the index ranges from 001 to 500. |\n| Remarks | A column in the original census reserved for the enumerators' observations or remarks. These have been transcribed in the original languages, mostly without translation to the other. Occasionally, the transcriber inserted a clarifying remark or made a translation in this column; these remarks or translations are specified with the transcriber's initials in braces: `{KMB}`. |\n\n### Supplementary materials\n\nThe [Supplementary Materials](../data/GlengarryVaudreuilSupplementary2.pdf)\ndescribe how the data was collected, how data cleaning was completed, how units\nof measure were standardized, and how the original data may be retrieved from\nLibrary and Archives Canada.\n\n## Questions\n\nMuch of the data includes measures of farm or household production, not only\nproduction from crops, but also from livestock. There are also data on\ninvestment---acres devoted to specific crops, numbers of livestock. Is it\npossible to create a model explaining variability in production for a specific\ncrop or product (wool, or cloth or butter)?\n\nFarming enterprises in this area in the nineteenth century were quite\ndiversified, and different farms made different choices as to what enterprise to\npursue. Conduct an analysis that aims to predict which households chose to\ninclude a specific crop (e.g., Barley or Rye) or product (linen or fulled\ncloth).\n\nThe enumerators who collected the data were generally educated and local to the\narea, and they were given fairly specific instructions. However, enumerators may\nhave made different decisions about how to collect the data. As an example:\nwhere detailed farm records may not have been kept, how was production measured?\nHow can one detect estimation in data? Are some variables more likely to be\naccurately measured than others? Be attentive to these issues and comment on how\nthese might affect your analysis results.\n\n## References\n\nGovernment of Canada (1855). *Census of the Canadas 1851-2, Vol II*. Lovell and\nLamoureux.\n<https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2012/statcan/rh-hc/CS98-1851-2-eng.pdf>\n\nIndividual records available through the [Census\nSearch](https://recherche-collection-search.bac-lac.gc.ca/eng/Census/index) from\nLibrary and Archives Canada. See *Supplementary Materials* for details on how to\nretrieve records and how the transcription process was completed. Data\ntranscribed from these records by Kenneth Morrison Brown.\n",
